  "body": "https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/tables.html#algorithm-for-processing-rows\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<table border=1>\r\n    <tbody>\r\n        <tr><td>a</td><td rowspan=2>b</td></tr>\r\n    </tbody>\r\n    <tbody>\r\n        <tr><td>a</td></tr>\r\n    </tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n```\r\n\r\nAccording to the spec ([step 13](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/tables.html#algorithm-for-processing-rows)), a table cell will unconditionally cover slots with a height equal to its rowspan.\r\n\r\nHowever, as is clear from the example above, `rowspan` does not go past row groups like `tbody`. Furthermore, I believe the recommendation in non-quirksmode for getting `rowspan=0`-like behavior is just doing something like `rowspan=1000`, which will with the current algorithm make a lot of extraneous table rows.\r\n\r\nThe current algorithm should be modified to use something like a \"list of incoming row spans\" (and perhaps use a -1 value for `rowspan=0`), with the algorithm basically saying \"if `incoming_rowspans[x_current]` is nonzero, fill this cell with the element covering the slot above it (`(x_current, y_current - 1)`) and decrement the value\" with special case handling for table model errors.\r\n\r\nWe then clear this list whenever we finish processing a group.",
